    <string name="app_name">Snoozemo</string>

    <!--
      Phase 1 debug screen only. This copy is scaffolding for a device check, not
      product copy: it is replaced by onboarding and settings in later phases and
      never reaches a release, so it is deliberately not translated and has not
      been through the propose-copy-in-chat step.
    -->
    <!--
      Screen titles and navigation (TODO.md Phase 4's screen split): MainScreen,
      PermissionsScreen (the interstitial) and SettingsScreen.
    -->
    <string name="permissions_title">Permissions</string>
    <string name="permissions_done">Done</string>
    <string name="settings_title">Settings</string>
    <!--
      MainScreen's Do Not Disturb banner. Reuses `setup_dnd_missing` for the body
      and `setup_action_allow` for the button, so the wording matches the same
      capability's row on PermissionsScreen exactly; only the title, which a row
      does not need, is its own string.
    -->
    <string name="dnd_banner_title">Do Not Disturb access needed</string>
    <!--
      The setup rows: a title, how the capability stands, and a button carrying
      the verb — but only while there is something left to do. A row whose
      capability is in place says so and offers nothing.

      Each action is a verb the user wants, not the route the tap takes. The old
      copy named the mechanism — `Opens Settings`, `Tap to allow`, `Tap to add`
      — which reads as a description of what is about to happen rather than an
      offer, and left an allowed row still looking like it wanted something.
      Every row says `Allow` and `Allowed`, whether the capability is a runtime
      prompt or, like Do Not Disturb access, a Settings toggle the user goes
      and flips (SPEC.md §5.2) — the mechanism differs but the wording doesn't
      need to.
    -->
    <string name="setup_dnd_title">Do Not Disturb access</string>
    <string name="setup_dnd_allowed">Allowed</string>
    <string name="setup_dnd_missing">Snoozemo can\'t snooze without it</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_title">Notifications</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_allowed">Allowed</string>
    <string name="setup_notifications_missing">Snoozemo can\'t show what a snooze is doing</string>
    <string name="setup_action_allow">Allow</string>
    <!--
      Every setup row's button says the same visible "Allow" (SPEC.md §5.2), so a
      screen reader announcing just that word can't tell a user which permission a
      given button opens once more than one row needs attention at once. This backs
      the accessible name instead — a formatted resource rather than Kotlin string
      concatenation, so a translation can reorder the action and the capability name
      independently of English word order (flagged by Codex on PR #103).
    -->
    <string name="setup_row_action_description">%1$s %2$s</string>
    <!--
      The location row (SPEC.md §3.2). Reuses `setup_action_allow` rather than
      a location-specific verb — "Allow" already says what the user gets, and
      a different verb per permission would be a distinction without a
      difference (AGENTS.md, *Concise copy*). `setup_location_allowed` reuses
      the same "Allowed" wording as notifications and Do Not Disturb access,
      for the same reason: every row pairs the `Allow` verb with the `Allowed`
      status, so the wording never depends on which mechanism grants it.
    -->
    <string name="setup_location_title">Location</string>
    <string name="setup_location_allowed">Allowed</string>
    <string name="setup_location_missing">Snoozemo can\'t tell when you\'ve left</string>
    <!--
      The calendar row (SPEC.md §4.3). Same `Allow` / `Allowed` pair as every
      other row. The missing line names what is lost rather than what is
      unread — this permission blocks nothing, it only removes the ongoing
      notification's third action.
    -->
    <!-- TODO: translate -->
    <string name="setup_calendar_title" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Calendar</string>
    <!-- TODO: translate -->
    <string name="setup_calendar_allowed" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Allowed</string>
    <!-- TODO: translate -->
    <string name="setup_calendar_missing" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Snoozemo can\'t offer your next meeting\'s end time</string>
    <!--
      The one disclosure this app shows (SPEC.md §12): a small dialog before
      the background-location prompt specifically, since that is the
      Play-restricted permission the declaration requires it precede — the
      foreground request has no dialog in front of it. Says what location is
      used for, that it only runs while a snooze is active, and that it never
      leaves the phone.
    -->
    <string name="location_background_rationale_title">Allow all the time</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_body">Snoozemo uses this to notice you\'ve left even while it\'s closed, so Do Not Disturb turns off on its own. It only runs while a snooze is active, and stays on your phone — nothing is collected or shared.</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_continue">Continue</string>
    <string name="location_background_rationale_dismiss">Not now</string>
    <string name="setup_tile_title">Quick Settings tile</string>
    <string name="setup_tile_missing">The one-tap way to snooze</string>
    <string name="setup_tile_added">Added</string>
    <!--
      The Filters row (TODO.md Phase 4): a shortcut to the system's own screen
      for editing Snoozemo's rule's interruption filter — which calls,
      messages, alarms and apps still break through. Distinct from
      `setup_dnd_title`, which allows changing zen state at all; this opens
      the *rule's own* configuration, previously reachable only by finding it
      in system DND settings by hand. Hidden entirely rather than shown
      disabled while DND access isn't allowed or no rule exists yet
      (`SettingsScreen`), so there is never a button that opens to
      nothing. `setup_action_add` isn't reused here — "Add" describes
      creating something, and this row edits a rule that already exists.
    -->
    <string name="setup_filters_title">Filters</string>
    <string name="setup_filters_status">What still gets through</string>
    <string name="setup_action_edit">Edit</string>
    <!--
      The debug log's switch (SPEC.md §4.6): on by default, and turning it off
      deletes what was kept. Copy is the maintainer's own wording (2026-08-22).
    -->
    <string name="setup_debug_log_title">Debug log</string>
    <string name="setup_debug_log_description">Save snooze details to help fix issues</string>
    <string name="setup_debug_log_save_failed">Couldn\'t save this setting</string>
    <!-- TODO: translate — reworded, so values-de is stale. State-neutral now:
         the flag outlives the Off toggle that set it, so a re-enable used to
         leave the switch reading On beside a message asserting Off (Codex,
         PR #153). It is also set at startup, where the switch may be either. -->
    <string name="setup_debug_log_cleanup_failed">Some saved files couldn\'t be deleted</string>
    <!--
      Crash reporting's switch (SPEC.md §12): off until turned on, `play` flavor
      only, and absent entirely on a build with no reporter behind it. The
      description says what leaves the phone, because that is the one thing
      this row is asking the user to agree to; the debug-log row above says
      nothing of the sort because nothing there leaves the phone by itself.
      TODO: translate
    -->
    <!-- TODO: translate — reworded because this row now governs analytics as
         well as crash reports (maintainer, 2026-08-31). It matches the consent
         card's title, minus the question mark: the card asks and the row
         labels, and one thing the user agreed to once should not carry two
         names. Named for what the user gets rather than for the two SDKs
         behind it, which made the row longer without telling anyone why they
         would want it on. -->
    <string name="setup_crash_reporting_title" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Help make Snoozemo better</string>
    <string name="setup_crash_reporting_description" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Send crash details and anonymous usage stats so bugs get fixed.</string>
    <string name="setup_crash_reporting_save_failed" tools:ignore="MissingTranslation">Couldn\'t save this setting</string>
    <!--
      The Play update banner (MainScreen and SettingsScreen, `play` flavor only — SPEC.md
      §3.4). Copy mirrors the sibling Simmo and Type Launcher repos' own
      banner wording, adapted for this app's name.
    -->
    <string name="play_update_banner_title">Update available</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_body">Get the latest Snoozemo from Google Play</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_update_button">Update</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_updating_title">Updating…</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_updating_body">Downloading from Google Play</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_downloaded_title">Update ready</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_downloaded_body">Restart Snoozemo to finish installing</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_restart_button">Restart</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_restart_failed">Couldn\'t restart to install</string>
    <string name="play_update_banner_dismiss">Dismiss</string>
    <!--
      The debug log's share action (SPEC.md §4.6): the share sheet plus a
      clipboard fallback, so a snooze that ended early or never ended can be
      reported. Reuses SetupRow's shape — title, status, a verb button — even
      though sharing is repeatable rather than a capability to fix once.
    -->
    <string name="setup_debug_log_share_title">Share debug logs</string>
    <string name="setup_debug_log_share_status">Recent snooze activity, to help fix a bug</string>
    <string name="setup_debug_log_share_action">Share</string>
    <!--
      Replaces the Share label on both Share affordances while one is
      actually running, so the disabled button says why it is disabled
      rather than just refusing the tap.
    -->
    <string name="share_in_progress">Sharing…</string>
    <string name="setup_debug_log_share_failed">Couldn\'t share the debug log</string>
    <!--
      The post-crash banner (SPEC.md §4.6): shown on SettingsScreen only while
      an uncaught-exception run is pinned. Share hands it to DebugReport, which
      picks the pinned run up automatically; Dismiss consumes the pin without
      sharing. Either way the pin is gone afterward — SPEC.md §4.6, "sharing
      consumes the pin".
    -->
    <string name="crash_banner_title">Snoozemo crashed</string>
    <string name="crash_banner_body">A debug log was saved on your device. Share it to help fix the bug? Nothing is sent until you choose where.</string>
    <string name="crash_banner_share">Share</string>
    <string name="crash_banner_dismiss">Dismiss</string>
    <!--
      A refused Dismiss (Codex, PR #89): the file layer couldn't consume the
      pin, so the banner stays and the tap needs an explanation rather than
      looking like it did nothing.
    -->
    <string name="crash_banner_dismiss_failed">Couldn\'t dismiss — try again</string>
    <!--
      The tile banner. The app screen keeps its own snooze button, but the tile
      is the way in (SPEC.md §4.2) — easier, and where people already go to
      silence a phone — so the screen leads with it rather than listing it as
      one option among equals.

      "Don't ask again" (maintainer, 2026-08-13), not "Not now": the dismissal
      is permanent and is not re-raised even when the tile is later removed, and
      "Not now" would promise the opposite. The row below the banner is the
      standing route, so saying it plainly costs the reminder and not the
      capability. Shorter than the "Don't show again" it replaces, and the more
      common idiom for a dismissal that sticks.
    -->
    <string name="tile_banner_title">Snooze from the shade</string>
    <string name="tile_banner_body">One tap, without unlocking. It\'s how Snoozemo is meant to be used.</string>
    <string name="tile_banner_add">Add tile</string>
    <string name="tile_banner_dismiss">Don\'t ask again</string>

    <!--
      The background-location banner (SPEC.md §8.1). Shown whenever the
      permission is missing, dismissible for good like the tile banner, and
      backed by the permanent location row on PermissionsScreen.

      Copy approved by the maintainer, 2026-08-31, after a first draft that
      led with the consequence ("Snoozes will run to the timer") and spent
      two sentences on the mechanism. An offer beats a warning here: the
      user has not lost anything, there is a capability they can switch on,
      and one line of benefit says it. The answers are worded as answers to
      the question, not as generic actions.
    -->
    <!-- TODO: translate -->
